Subject: [PATCH] Save states: a game's own word in the layout check (PS2Runtime::setStateLayoutSalt)

A game build can change what a save state holds in ways the runtime cannot see: code patched at recompile time
that sizes the game's own arrays differently (Transformers' sound emitters, 35 -> 256, N7 step 3). A state made
before such a change would load onto arrays of another size. PS2Runtime::setStateLayoutSalt(u64): set once at
start, before any state loads, it is folded into the state's layout fingerprint - a state made with another value
is refused like one of another RAM size (the game runs on, or boots fresh, with the message on screen). 0, the
default, leaves the fingerprint as before: states of games that never set it still load.

ps2xRuntime:
- ps2_runtime.h/.cpp: setStateLayoutSalt / stateLayoutSalt.
- ps2_savestate.cpp: layoutFingerprint folds in a non-zero salt.
diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime.h b/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime.h
index c22971e..20b6d25 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime.h
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/ps2_runtime.h
@@ -460,6 +460,11 @@ public:
         uint32_t addedDataEnd;
     };
     static const GuestLayout &guestLayout();
+    // A game's own word in the save state's layout check (ps2_savestate.cpp): what its build changed that the
+    // runtime cannot see - code patched at recompile time that sizes the game's own arrays differently, say. Set
+    // once at start, before any state loads; a state made with another value is refused (another layout).
+    static void setStateLayoutSalt(uint64_t salt);
+    static uint64_t stateLayoutSalt();
     // The largest free block of the guest heap (what a malloc of that size or less finds).
     uint32_t guestHeapLargestFree() const;
     uint32_t reserveAsyncCallbackStack(uint32_t size, uint32_t alignment = 16u);
diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/ps2_runtime.cpp b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/ps2_runtime.cpp
index 6d96ec2..f743ea0 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/ps2_runtime.cpp
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/ps2_runtime.cpp
@@ -2578,6 +2578,21 @@ void PS2Runtime::resetGuestHeapLocked(uint32_t guestBase, uint32_t guestLimit)
     m_guestHeapConfigured = true;
 }
 
+namespace
+{
+    uint64_t g_stateLayoutSalt = 0u;
+}
+
+void PS2Runtime::setStateLayoutSalt(uint64_t salt)
+{
+    g_stateLayoutSalt = salt;
+}
+
+uint64_t PS2Runtime::stateLayoutSalt()
+{
+    return g_stateLayoutSalt;
+}
+
 const PS2Runtime::GuestLayout &PS2Runtime::guestLayout()
 {
     static const GuestLayout layout = []()
diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/ps2_savestate.cpp b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/ps2_savestate.cpp
index a27a84b..8aa8da6 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/ps2_savestate.cpp
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/ps2_savestate.cpp
@@ -83,6 +83,9 @@ namespace
         // (N7) The game's view of main RAM (PS2Runtime::guestLayout, PS2X_GUEST_RAM_MB): the same build's 32 MB and
         // 256 MB modes put the heap and the port's added data in different places, so a state of one is not the other's.
         hash = (hash ^ PS2Runtime::guestLayout().ramMb) * 1099511628211ull;
+        // (the game's own word: PS2Runtime::setStateLayoutSalt - 0, no change, unless the game sets one)
+        if (const uint64_t salt = PS2Runtime::stateLayoutSalt(); salt != 0u)
+            hash = (hash ^ salt) * 1099511628211ull;
         return hash;
     }
 
